<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guantánamo Bay Naval Base</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guantánamo Bay Naval Base is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> naval base on Guantánamo Bay in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, leased under an agreement of 1903 that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a>. A treaty of 1934 made the lease permanent unless both parties agree to end it, and the Cuban government has rejected it since 1959. Since 2002 the base has held a detention camp for prisoners captured in the 'war on terror'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guantanamo-bay-naval-base.jpg" alt="Guantánamo Bay Naval Base" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by Chief Mass Communication Specialist Bill Mesta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Navy_100506-N-8241M-191_An_aerial_view_of_Bulkeley_Hall_at_Naval_Station_Guantanamo_Bay,_Cuba._Bulkeley_Hall_is_the_naval_station_headquarters_and_administration_building.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The base occupies 45 square miles (117 km2) of land and water on the shore of Guantánamo Bay, at the south-eastern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. It is the oldest overseas American naval base. The bay divides the station into sections: Leeward Point holds the airfield, while Windward Point holds most of the base's activities. The highest point is John Paul Jones Hill, at 495 feet (151 m), and many coves and peninsulas along the shore provide anchorage for ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was first inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno people</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> arrived on 30 April 1494, on his second voyage, spent the night and named the bay Puerto Grande. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a> a British expeditionary force held the bay briefly and renamed it Cumberland Bay, but withdrew after an attempt to march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> was repulsed by Spanish troops. In 1898, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, the American fleet attacking Santiago secured the harbour for protection during the hurricane season, and marines landed with naval support.</p><p>The war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1898. In 1901 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a> provided that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> would sell or lease to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> lands for coaling or naval stations. After initial resistance by the Cuban Constitutional Convention it was incorporated into the Cuban constitution, which took effect in 1902, and land at Guantánamo Bay was granted the following year. The lease of 1903 has no fixed expiration date. It left ultimate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> with Cuba while giving the United States sole jurisdiction during its occupation, at a rent of $2,000 a year in gold coin.</p><p>Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> the Cuban government has protested against the American presence, arguing that the base was imposed by force and is illegal under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>; only one rent payment has been accepted since 1959. In 1961 both sides completed minefields around the perimeter, some 55,000 mines in all, and at least ten people died in explosions between 1961 and 1965. President Bill Clinton ordered the American field cleared in 1996. In 1964 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> stopped the flow of water to the base, and the commander had the pipelines cut. Water was first imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> by barge, and a desalination plant was then moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guantanamo Bay Naval Base” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The convention was forced to adopt the Platt Amendment, which gave the United States a right to intervene and a naval base at <b>Guantánamo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-military-government-in-cuba">United States Military Government in Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
